  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 125 AM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nThe ridge begins to flatten Wednesday as several waves begin to \nmake their way through the northern flow. Most models (80-90%) \ndepict Wednesday as hot and dry like previous days, though a few \nensemble members (~10%) suggest that we could see thunderstorms \napproach northern Oklahoma from Kansas.\n\nThursday through the end of the week redevelops the ridge over the\nwestern conus, putting us in the path of several waves in the\nupper flow. This will bring back low chances for rain (10-30%) and\na chance for some relief from the heat. High temperature\nuncertainty jumps on Thursday across northern Oklahoma--where the\nmiddle 50% (interquartile range) of potential outcomes spans a\n10-degree range--before spreading farther south toward the\nweekend. Even with the potential cooling, the likelihood of daily\nhighs reaching or exceeding 100 degrees remains moderate to high\n(50-80% probability, highest in north Texas) through Saturday. Key\nfactors determining whether relief arrives and any rain chances\nwill be the position / strength of the ridge and timing of\napproaching upper level waves.\n\nDay",
